<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jules Ferry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jules Ferry was a French republican statesman who twice served as prime minister, in 1880–1881 and 1883–1885. As education minister his laws of 1881–1882 made primary schooling free, compulsory and secular. He was also the leading advocate of colonial expansion, pursuing the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, Tonkin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, and fell from power in 1885 after the French reverse at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jules François Camille Ferry was born on 5 April 1832 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> department, the son of Charles-Édouard Ferry, a lawyer from a family of bellmakers established at Saint-Dié, and Adélaïde Jamelet. His grandfather had been mayor of Saint-Dié through the Consulate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">First Empire</a>. He studied law and was called to the bar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1854, but turned to politics and wrote for newspapers, especially Le Temps, attacking the Second Empire and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-eugene-haussmann">Baron Haussmann</a>; his articles were republished in 1868 as The Fantastic Tales of Haussmann.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected republican deputy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1869, he protested against the declaration of war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and on 6 September 1870 the Government of National Defence made him prefect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>. He administered Paris during the siege and resigned on 5 June 1871 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>. After a spell as minister at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> he returned as deputy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and became a leader of the Opportunist Republicans. He sat in ministries from 4 February 1879 and was prime minister in 1880–1881 and 1883–1885.</p><p>Ferry sought a non-clerical public education. After a law of 27 February 1880 reorganised the committee of public education, he passed his laws of 16 June 1881 and 28 March 1882, which made primary education free, secular and mandatory; the professors called the Republic's black hussars doubled in number under his ministry. He also formed the idea after 1870 of a great colonial empire, chiefly for economic exploitation. He directed the negotiations for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1881, prepared a treaty on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and organised the conquest of Annam and Tonkin. On 28 March 1884 he told the Chamber that the superior races had a right, because they had a duty, to civilise the inferior races.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> over Tonkin ended his premiership. The sudden retreat of French troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a> caused uproar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the Tonkin Affair, with violent denunciation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> and other radicals, and he fell on 30 March 1885. The peace treaty of 9 June 1885, by which the Qing ceded suzerainty over Annam and Tonkin, was the work of his ministry, but he never served as premier again.</p><p>After the resignation of Jules Grévy in December 1887 he stood for the presidency but withdrew in favour of Sadi Carnot when the radicals refused support. On 10 December 1887 a man named Aubertin tried to assassinate him, and he died on 17 March 1893 of complications attributed to the wound. The Chamber of Deputies gave him a state funeral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jules Ferry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the expansionist <b>Jules Ferry</b> the republic acquired Indochina, Madagascar, vast territories in West and Central Africa and much of Polynesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Social Darwinism and theories of race gave it a supposedly rational basis: in 1883 <b>Jules Ferry</b> declared that “Superior races have a right, because they have a duty.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jules Ferry</b>, prime minister from 1880 to 1885, enacted the Ferry Laws, ending the Catholic Church’s part in many state institutions, including schools.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jules Ferry's</b> laws made primary schooling free, compulsory and secular, and the 1905 law separated church and state.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-third-republic">French Third Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-Dié-des-Vosges, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in 8th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.28,6.95&amp;year=1871" aria-label="Saint-Dié-des-Vosges on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4711 475 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jules Ferry"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5161 745h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5161 745h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.28,6.95&amp;year=1871">What was here in 1871? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
